{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "Traveling Without Moving", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "In the Dreamtime", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 3, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 5, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.2", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Describe detailed facts about events or actions in work and social contexts, in written or spoken situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "pelican ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a large water bird that eats fish", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The Wangkumara people have recently sent a pelican to explore another part of the country. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": " These are words you use to tell a Dreamtime story.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "pouch", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a soft bag of skin", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Pelican has just flown out across the land with fish in the pouch under his beak.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "discovery", "type": "name"}, {"content": "something found for the first time", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The people have asked him not to stop during his journey of discovery. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "opal", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a semi-precious milky stone that flashes color", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: What he is seeing is opal.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to peck", "type": "name"}, {"content": " to bite with a beak", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Pelican has now begun pecking at the colored stones with his beak.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud05", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "spark ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a small bit of live fire", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: All of a sudden, a spark flies.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud06", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "eventually", "type": "name"}, {"content": "after a long time", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adverb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The flames spread for miles, eventually reaching a group of Wangkumara Aborigines camping by a creek.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03aud07", "type": "audio"}]}, {"content": "engenn10le05ob03re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " The Passive Gerund", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the passive form of a gerund to emphasize that the subject of the sentence is being acted upon.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Look at the following example:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">Our legends <span class=\"strong\">being brought</span> to life and <span class=\"strong\">being passed on</span> help to keep us centered as a people. </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Here, the focus is on the legends, the receiver of the action. The agent, or \u2018doer', of the action (in this case, \u2018people') is not the focus. It is understood by the context of the writing that the \u2018doer' of the action (bring to life, pass on) are people and therefore not important.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Using passive forms of gerunds adds variety and depth to your writing. This style of writing is used often in stories, articles and academic texts.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Notice the structure of the passive gerund:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"italic\">to be</span> +<span class=\"italic\">-ing</span> + past participle", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Notice the passive gerund in the following sentences.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The Aboriginal elder, Ngaka, of the Wangkumara Aboriginal group said that there was a Dreamtime legend being told that day.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "\u201cThe story of how opal stones gave us the gift of fire  being recounted here is important\u201d, he said. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Being reminded of our stories is part of Aboriginal life. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Now a fire is being lit by the sparks in the dry grass nearby.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Soon, meat and fish being roasted in the fire is usual for Wangkumara families.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": " Present Perfect for Unusual Events", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You can use the Present Perfect tense to talk about unusual events since you use this tense to connect past events with present situations. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "You use the Present Perfect tense:<br/><br/>  <ul><li>For something that started in the past and continues in the present:<br/><span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">They've been married</span> for almost thirty years.<br/> <span class=\"strong\">He has lived</span> in Toronto all his life.</span></li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<ul><li>For something that has only recently happened:</li><br/><span class=\"italic\">Pelican has <span class=\"strong\">just</span> flown out across the land.<br/> He has <span class=\"strong\">just</span> now begun to tell the story. <br/> The Wangkumara people have <span class=\"strong\">recently</span> sent a pelican to explore another part of the country.</span></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use the words <span class=\"italic\">just</span>, <span class=\"italic\">only just</span> and <span class=\"italic\">recently</span> in these contexts.<br/><br/> <ul><li>When we are talking about our experience up to the present:<br/><span class=\"italic\">My last holiday was the worst holiday I <span class=\"strong\">have ever had</span>.<br/> <span class=\"strong\">Have you ever seen</span> a fire before?<br/> No, <span class=\"strong\">I've never seen</span> a fire.</span></li></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You often use the adverb <span class=\"italic\">ever</span> to talk about experience up to the present and you use <span class=\"italic\">never</span> for the negative form<br/><br/>  <ul><li>for something that happened in the past but is important at the time of speaking:</li><span class=\"italic\">I can't get in the house. I've lost my keys.</span></ul>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Take a look at the examples from the text: ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "He has just now begun to tell the story.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Wangkumara people have recently sent a pelican to explore another part of the country. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Pelican has just flown out across the land with fish in the pouch under his beak.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Have you ever needed to stop? ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Well this is just what has happened to Pelican. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Pelican has now begun pecking at the colored stones with his beak.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Have the people ever seen fire before?", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "Go online or to the library to find pictures of Australian Aboriginal Dreamtime paintings.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Look at them and take some notes.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Consider the colors and images in the paintings.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Look at the movement and subject matter.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Read some background on the paintings or artists.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Note any good new vocabulary words you can use to talk about the paintings.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share what you have found out with a partner or a group.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I find pictures of Australian Aboriginal Dreamtime paintings.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I take some notes on the painting, considering color, movement, subject matter and images.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I find out the meaning of new vocabulary.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I describe the paintings to a partner or group.", "type": "text"}]}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "type": "audio", "title": "", "_data": [], "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"audiosrc": "engenn10le05ob03re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn10le05ob03re2img02.jpg", "images": [{"image": "engenn10le05ob03re2img02.jpg", "text": "", "time": "0", "id": 0}, {"image": "engenn10le05ob03re2img02.jpg", "text": "The Aboriginal elder, Ngaka, of the Wangkumara Aboriginal group said that there was a Dreamtime legend being told that day. \"The story of how opal stones gave us the gift of fire  being recounted here is important\", he said. Being reminded of our stories is part of Aboriginal life. Our legends being brought to life and being passed on help to keep us centered as a people. <br/>Ngaka stands. He has just now begun to tell the story.  \"It's thousands of years ago,\" he says, \"and the Wangkumara people have recently sent a pelican to explore another part of the country. <br/>Pelican has just flown out across the land with fish in the pouch under his beak which is full of water to keep the fish fresh. He needs the fish because the Wangkumara people have asked him not to stop during his journey of discovery.\"", "time": "100", "id": 1}, {"image": "engenn10le05ob03re2img01.jpg", "text": "Ngaka continues. \"Pelican feels sick and so, just now, he has decided to rest on top of a high hill. Have you ever needed to stop because your body is unhappy about not  having rested? Well, this is just what has happened to Pelican. Then, while he's looking out over the land, he notices magnificent colors on the terrain below. What he is seeing is opal.\"<br/>\"Being curious,\" Ngaka says, smiling, \"Pelican has now begun pecking at the colored stones with his beak. All of a sudden, a spark flies. Now a fire is being lit by the sparks in the dry grass nearby. The flames spread for miles, eventually reaching a group of Wangkumara Aborigines camping by the creek.\"", "time": "55000", "id": 2}, {"image": "engenn10le05ob03re2img03.jpg", "text": "\"Have the people ever seen fire before? No, they have not! Soon they realize they can cook their food with this fire. Soon, meat and fish being roasted in the fire is usual for Wangkumara families.\u201d<br/>\"We always need reminding,\" says Ngaka, \"of this gift that has come into our possession.\"", "time": "107000", "id": 3}]}}], "id": 1}], "exercises": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [{"type": "instructions", "contents": [{"content": " I can talk about different cultures, myths and legends.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can talk about unusual events in the past. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": " I can report facts about events or actions. ", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "What myths or legends do you know from Colombia?", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": "Do you think it is important to pass on myths and legends through generations? Why?", "type": "Free Header Item"}]}, {"type": "group", "contents": [{"content": "Welcome", "type": "group_name"}]}, {"content": {"allowed_attempts": 2, "incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "try again", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next 